        "design_notes": "Human-feedback revision of L10 v2. The redundant left crate is replaced by wall, the left-side activity column is compressed, and the unused upper-right space is walled off while preserving the same 13-step core witness. The player first pushes a crate into S side to trigger box_to_sticky and sticky_merge, then moves B/S twice: once into the upper channel and once to trigger sticky_to_box. The cut crate is pushed down, and the remaining sticky pair is moved onto the target. Complete graph analysis reports 178 reachable states and 423 legal transitions, down from v2's 221 / 546, with the same shortest input sequence. Core event probe proves all wins require B/S shift, box_to_sticky, sticky_merge, sticky_to_box, sticky rigid movement, and crate push. Order probe proves no winning path shifts B/S before sticky_merge; count probe proves all wins require at least two B/S shifts; reachable scan reports no P/L hit. Independent review_2 evidence reviewer returned supports_claim / required_action:none; puzzle critic returned supports_with_noncore_caveats / required_action:none. Caveat: the chain is still strongly forced and should be playtested as a compact L10 timing application, not as an open scheduling puzzle.\n",
